// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// Docs only: decision D12 (big arena chunks are kept for reuse), chat 10. 4 anchored edits over 2 files (docs/LLVM_POC.md,
// docs/LLVM_ROADMAP.md); each anchor occurs exactly once on master and none starts at the beginning of a line. The code and
// docs/langbench numbers are in the tarball ubel-llvm-zzchunk-19.tar.gz; apply the two in either order.
//
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right only because that one (D1 v2, mine) is applied on master: check that first.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "Retaining one big chunk in the runtime (tried, not delivered) took the run to 0.40 s with 12,362 faults, close to Rust here.", "Keeping big chunks for reuse in the runtime (adopted in chat 10, decision D12, see the log) took the run to about 0.5 s with 12,363 faults, level with Rust here.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "Whether to adopt that retention is open (LANGBENCH.md, Ubel MID in the sandbox).", "See LANGBENCH.md, Ubel MID in the sandbox.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "- 2026-11-26 (chat 10): **D1 v2, first step: sema tags the result of a MID call", "- 2026-11-27 (chat 10): **Big arena chunks are kept for reuse (decision D12, provisional).** The owner chose to adopt it. `ubel_rt.c`: when an arena ends, its chunks of 1 MiB or more (up to four in all) are kept instead of freed, and a new arena takes one only if its size is exactly the size it asks for; `UBEL_ARENA_CACHE=0` turns it off; `ubel_arena_big_reuses()` is a test and statistics hook (`ubel_rt.h`). Why: the arena benchmark spent three quarters of its time in the kernel (820,635 page faults, a fresh 512 MB chunk mapped and unmapped every round). Result in the sandbox: 1.01x of Rust (497.6 ms against 494.4 ms), from 5.2x, with 12,363 faults; peak memory 49.9 MB against 34.0 MB. `rt_gc_test.c`: two new tests (19 in all): reuse and its limits (exactly 1 MiB is kept, 16 bytes less is not, another size is never served, the cap is four, the same chunk comes back) and the off switch. Verified on a fresh clone of master 5f6160b with the tarball ubel-llvm-zzchunk-19 applied by the real applier: core lib 232, ubel_llvm 6 unit and 19 integration, the CLI suites and doc-tests ok; `run_equiv.sh` 139 passed and 0 failed at 64-bit and with `-m32`, the runtime tests 19 and 0 failed at -O0, -O2 and under ASan and UBSan on both widths; `run_ubel_build.sh` 74 supported programs, 58 intentional refusals, 217 `err_` rejected, 33 of 203 `ok_` built (unchanged); GC stress 1 with ASan and UBSan on the arena fixtures and the benchmark; langbench `--size small` correct, ubel-mid included. A kept chunk holds stale bytes, so a throwaway experiment treated every arena chunk as cacheable, filled each reused one with 0xA5 and ran the equivalence suite: every example result was unchanged (the only failures were the new cap test objecting to the experiment's own constants). Mutation testing by hand, 11 mutants of the cache (reuse off, any larger chunk, cap off by one, the 1 MiB boundary at install and at destroy, the knob ignored, the knob ignored at destroy, the counter, the size count, the chunk left in the list, nothing put back): all caught. Not decided: a cap on retained bytes, giving kept chunks back under memory pressure, serving a request from a larger chunk, thread safety (the cache is as unsynchronised as the 64 KiB one). Not verified: the hosted runner, ARM, macOS, Termux.\n- 2026-11-26 (chat 10): **D1 v2, first step: sema tags the result of a MID call")
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_ROADMAP.md", "(B2 item 4: specify or remove).", "(B2 item 4: specify or remove). |\n| D12 | Do big arena chunks stay mapped for reuse after their arena ends? | B2, the arena benchmark row | DECIDED by the owner (2026-11-27), provisional: yes. The runtime keeps up to four chunks of 1 MiB or more and hands one to a new arena only when its size is exactly the size asked for; `UBEL_ARENA_CACHE=0` turns it off. Measured in the sandbox: the arena binary-trees benchmark went from 5.2x to 1.01x of Rust (page faults 820,635 to 12,363), and its peak memory rose from 34 to 50 MB. Not decided: a cap on retained bytes, giving kept chunks back under memory pressure, serving a request from a larger chunk, thread safety (the cache is as unsynchronised as the 64 KiB one).")
)
